<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · infrastructure</p><h1 class="enc-title">Siloam tunnel</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 700 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century-bce">8th century BCE</a> · Also known as Hezekiah's tunnel</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Siloam tunnel, also called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hezekiah">Hezekiah</a>'s tunnel, is an ancient water channel about 533 metres long cut through the rock beneath the City of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>. It carries water from the Gihon spring to the Pool of Siloam and is usually dated to King Hezekiah's preparations for the Assyrian siege of 701 BCE. An inscription found in it in 1880 describes the two teams of diggers meeting.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/siloam-tunnel.jpg" alt="Siloam tunnel" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tamar Hayardeni תמר הירדני · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Siloam22.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> stands on a mountain and is naturally defensible, but its main source of fresh water, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gihon-spring">Gihon Spring</a>, lies on the side of the cliff above the Kidron Valley, so a defensible wall would leave the spring outside. The Canaanites removed this weakness by building a strongly fortified tower around the spring and linking it to the city walls by a wall carrying a protected corridor. A Canaanite tunnel had collected the spring water since about 1800 BCE and led it south through the bedrock to fields in the Kidron Valley.</p><p>The best-known explanation of the Siloam tunnel is that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hezekiah">Hezekiah</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judah">Judah</a>, fearing a siege by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyrians</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sennacherib">Sennacherib</a>, diverted the spring's water into the city. The Bible says he blocked the upper outlet of the Gihon spring and channelled the water down to the west side of the city (2 Chronicles 32:30).</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The curving tunnel runs from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gihon-spring">Gihon Spring</a> to the Pool of Siloam and is 533 metres long. The engineers moved the water by using a height difference of about 30 centimetres between the two ends, a gradient of 0.06 per cent. According to the Siloam inscription, the tunnel was carved out of the living rock by two teams, one starting at each end and meeting in the middle. The inscription is partly unreadable, and several directional errors are visible in the tunnel itself.</p><p>Aharon Horovitz interprets the tunnel as a means of keeping the whole outflow of the spring inside the walls, to keep water from a besieging army. Both the spring and the pool would have been used by the inhabitants as water sources, whereas the earlier channel had released unused water into the Kidron Valley, where besiegers could have taken it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>Franciscus Quaresmius first described the tunnel in modern times, in 1625. Edward Robinson explored it in 1838 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-warren">Charles Warren</a> in 1865. Neither Quaresmius nor Robinson linked it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hezekiah">Hezekiah</a>, but in 1871 Warren suggested the Pool of Siloam may have been dug by him, and later wrote that the inscription agreed well with the biblical account.</p><p>Radiocarbon dating of organic matter in the plaster and uranium-thorium dating of speleothems supported a date in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hezekiah">Hezekiah</a>'s reign. Excavations by Ronny Reich and Eli Shukron cast doubt on it in 2011, and they suggested a date several decades earlier, in the last part of the 9th century or early 8th century BCE. De Groot and Fadida support this on the basis of pottery. They note that the biblical passage on Hezekiah's waterworks names no place in the city and may refer to waterworks in the Mamilla area.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Siloam tunnel” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Siloam tunnel</b> under Jerusalem, over 500 metres long, was dug from both ends; an inscription records the moment the two teams of diggers met.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-judah">Kingdom of Judah · At a glance</a></li></ul></section>
